      "handbookUrl": "https://programsandcourses.anu.edu.au/course/ARTV2751",
      "summary": "In this course students extend their technical and theoretical skills in planographic printing (screenprinting and lithography) via set projects and conceptual prompts. Students apply these skills to critically examine printmedia's key position in contemporary art. Studio projects explore the material possibilities of print, through colour, halftone and autographic mark making. In so doing, students can connect histories of print to specific visual effects and form. By reflecting on and experimenting with these forms and lineages, students will create work that uses print in context and dialogue with contemporary art. Students may complete this course twice for a maximum credit value of 12 units, provided they enrol in a different topic in each instance/semester. Please note that the course content, assessment structure, and reading list will change depending on the topic and the expertise of the lecturer convening the course. Please refer to the class summary for the specific term in which you wish to enrol for a detailed description. Topics may include, but are not limited to, the following: Fashioning with PrintThis course explores the role of printmaking (screenprinting and/or lithography) in contemporary practice. Technical and conceptual skills developed in ARTV1751 will be extended by critically engaging with print operations specific to chosen mediums and in context to print histories of transmission, amplification and reproduction. Through studio investigations, lectures and group discussion, students will fashion printed works which critically and creatively engage with processes that connect and underpin contemporary visual culture. Proliferation in Print",
