      "type": "course",
      "code": "BAPA3004",
      "name": "Living with Giants: Australia's Security in a Contested Asia",
      "college": "ANU College of Asia and the Pacific",
      "school": "Strategic and Defence Studies Centre",
      "schoolCode": "CAP",
      "level": "UGRD",
      "terms": [
        "Spring Session"
      ],
      "conveners": [],
      "handbookUrl": "https://programsandcourses.anu.edu.au/course/BAPA3004",
      "summary": "The 2020s have seen the rise of numerous strategic problems for Australia. There are giant states in fierce competition, such as the United States and China, and emerging giants in India and Indonesia. There are also problems from below, such as climate change, artificial intelligence, cyber security, and terrorism. This course examines the security challenges facing Australia and explores how Australia should approach its region. This course is designed with a focus on active and authentic classroom experiences, with a heavy emphasis on simulation exercises that replicate real world problem solving. The course will thus help any students wishing to understand how the Australian government pursues national security, whether they are seeking a future career in government, or to contribute analysis to policy and public debates about how Australia should seek its security in a contested Asia.",
