      "handbookUrl": "https://programsandcourses.anu.edu.au/course/BURM1002",
      "summary": "Burmese is the official language of Myanmar and serves as the main lingua franca in the country as well as in Burmese communities worldwide. Myanmar embraces many ethnic groups and a diversity of cultures while being predominantly a Buddhist country. Burmese has a long history of use in literary works and music, and today the language serves as a key medium connecting Myanmar communities domestically and internationally via the internet. Students will develop an understanding of basic cultural practices in Myanmar society through this course. In this course, students will be introduced to spoken-style Burmese language and gain communicative competence at an introductory level. The course emphasizes conversations in everyday situations such as greetings, shopping, family gatherings, describing hobbies, expressing likes and dislikes, and making reservations. Students will also learn how to use the language appropriately in different social situations while familiarizing themselves with reading and writing Burmese script. Students will study short conversations and texts, learning the main aspects of phonology, pronunciation, grammar, and vocabulary of Burmese. Students are given opportunities to practice their Burmese listening, speaking, writing, reading, and translation skills Throughout the semester, students will role-play conversations and occasionally interact with Burmese guest speakers who share their lived experiences of their own places and cultures to enhance students' receptive, interactive, and productive skills.",
