      "handbookUrl": "https://programsandcourses.anu.edu.au/course/ENGN6626",
      "summary": "This course presents the principles and techniques fundamental to the analysis and design of digital communication systems. It focuses on the basic building blocks of a digital communication system (source encoder/decoder, channel encoder/decoder, digital modulator/demodulator and channel characteristics). The emphasis is on mathematical underpinnings of communications theory along with practical applications and emerging machine learning tools. Specific topics include: • Review of Probability and Random Processes and Modelling of Gaussian noise process. • Digital Modulation Techniques: Signal space analysis, BPSK, QPSK, QAM. • Digital Demodulation & Detection Techniques: Correlator-demodulator, Maximum likelihood detection (MLD) in additive white Gaussian noise (AWGN), bit error rate (BER) performance. • Channel Encoder/Decoder: Linear block codes, Cyclic codes, Convolutional codes, Viterbi algorithm. • Information Theory and Source Coding: Source Entropy, Huffman Coding, Channel Capacity. Matlab and/or Python-based programming environment is used in the analysis and design.",
