      "summary": "This advanced undergraduate course enables students to study a range of exciting and important historical topics in collaboration with specialists in the field. The topics will vary from year to year, depending on the expertise of the convenor. The structure and assessment of the course may vary from topic to topic. In each iteration the course will enable students to develop their knowledge and research skills by working with experts. Students will have an opportunity to develop a research project of their own, with guidance and support. Please refer to the Class tab for details of the specific topic available each year, and the Class Summary for a detailed description including convenor, theme, format, scheduling, detailed assessment structure, and indicative readings. Sample topics may include, but are not limited to, the following: The History of CapitalismThe History of HumanitarianismGender and HistoryThe Death Penalty and its Alternatives in History Topic for Semester One, 2026: Fame and Celebrity: Historical Perspectives Fame and Celebrity: Historical Perspectives is an advanced undergraduate course exploring the history of celebrity – one of the most intriguing cultural phenomena of modernity – in the Western world. Drawing on interdisciplinary perspectives, the course will consider a range of theoretical approaches to celebrity as a phenomenon, and explore the historical roots and development of a condition that has sometimes been viewed as an intrinsically recent one. Along the way, we will reflect on the manifestations of celebrity in different time periods and diverse contexts, from royalty to politics to entertainment; address the intersection of celebrity with historical shifts such as the emergence of the mass media and the rise of individualism; and consider how ideas of gender, class, race, nation, or other aspects of identity have shaped and been implicated in particular forms of celebrity. All students will come prepared having done the weekly readings, which individual students will take the lead in presenting during the weekly seminar. The major piece of assessment is a research essay of 2,500 words on a topic agreed with the convener, and for which a plan will be submitted for review and assessment during Week 7.",
