      "handbookUrl": "https://programsandcourses.anu.edu.au/course/LAWS8241",
      "summary": "This course reviews the ever-changing scope and operation of the executive power inherent in the Australian Constitution. It is set against the background of the UK development of the concept, largely by the courts, but its primary focus is on section 61 of the Constitution and relevant decisions of the High Court. Given the significant expansion of executive power in recent years, the course also explores contemporary issues associated with migration and counter-terrorism powers, as well as the trend towards republicanising the legal system. The course adopts a historical, analytical, and topical approach to enable a deeper understanding of the concept of executive power in Australia. Key topics include: the emergence of the concept of an Australian executive power and the consequential progressive reduction in reliance on prerogative power as a useful concept;the conventions and law governing the exercise of executive power (by Governor-General, Ministers, Cabinet and administrators);the constitutional grounding of responsible government and its effectiveness in the 21st century;the narrowing of the traditional immunities and privileges the common law extends to the Crown and the impact of the practice by the executive of privatising and contracting out;federal issues relating to executive power, including the issues that arise if there is a conflict between the exercise of Commonwealth and State executive power;the unique position of the executive in the ACT; andchallenges to the protection of individuals through the rule of law, using counter-terrorism law and migration law as examples. Students will engage with readings, research, and interactive learning activities designed to encourage critical analysis of executive power and its implications in contemporary Australia.",
