      "summary": "This course is intended to help students to develop an understanding of the structure and principles of international investment treaty law and arbitration. We will examine the development of this area of international law, its main substantive principles, and investor-State dispute settlement. The course introduces the key components of public international law related to the protection of foreign investment, and considers the procedural means by which disputes about those protections are settled. Topics that may be covered include: Historical background and sources of international investment law.Standards of investment protection.State responses to investment claims.Remedies and enforcement.The future of investment treaty law and arbitration. To develop knowledge and skills, students will engage in a range of learning activities, including interactive seminars which give students the opportunity to develop their oral communication skills through discussions and resolve complex problems through analysis of scenarios. The course is also designed to develop research and writing skills.",
