      "handbookUrl": "https://programsandcourses.anu.edu.au/course/LAWS8587",
      "summary": "This introductory course is an essential element in gaining an understanding of Australia's legal system. It explores the main elements of public and private law that make up the Australian Legal system with an emphasis on how those principles apply to the role of government. The course is designed to build on the understanding of the Australian legal system introduced in the Law and Legal Institutions course. Students completing this course will have acquired sufficient knowledge and skills to enable them to enrol in the other more specialist courses in government and regulation. The course will include: concepts of regulation and mechanisms used to achieve regulatory outcomes;finding and interpreting regulatory sources, including legislation, industry codes, and court judgements; and anticipating or recognising their regulatory intent; andAn understanding of the influence of underlying constitutional and institutional frameworks in which those regulatory sources operate, including the role of private law.To develop their knowledge and skills, students will engage in discussions, analyse judgements and develop the skills to analyse and communicate outcomes of research.",
