      "handbookUrl": "https://programsandcourses.anu.edu.au/course/SCOM6027",
      "summary": "The interface between evidence and political decision making is of fundamental importance to modern society. Yet while our techniques of enquiry have allowed us to learn ever more about the world - and our collective ability to enact change has become ever more powerful - the relationship between evidence and action has never been entirely smooth. Indeed, things may even be getting worse. This course traces the dynamics, contours and fractures of the interface between science and politics. Topics covered include: uses of science in political decision making; differing attitudes to scientific advice and the values underpinning them; ways of countering hostility to science; the role of new technology in changing the dynamics of the relationship between science and politics. Note: This course is co-taught with undergraduate students but assessed separately.",
