      "summary": "This interdisciplinary course will introduce students to contemporary understandings of addiction and drug and alcohol use, from a socio-cultural perspective. It addresses the question of why addiction and drug use have such prominence as medical and social problems in contemporary Western cultures and provides a range of concepts and tools for understanding their significance. The course examines alcohol and drug use as socio-cultural practices which occur within specific social contexts. The course will also investigate the continued debates about 'what' addiction is and what these debates reveal about medical science and conceptions of health. The significance of gender, race, age and class in structuring patterns and experiences of drug use will be explored.",
